package cosmonic:agent@0.5.0;

/// The caller may load a toolset into itself while it runs. Not published;
/// for discussion.
///
/// Spawning is declarative: a child's toolsets are fixed when it starts. This
/// interface is the separate, explicit power to change a running agent's own
/// toolsets. A world that does not import it cannot load anything, and a
/// spawned child never receives it in this version. A parent cannot load a
/// toolset into a child it spawned; it spawns a new child with different
/// toolsets instead.
///
/// A loaded toolset is a component with its own grants, started by the
/// implementer. The implementer offers its tool definitions through the
/// caller's own `cosmonic:agent/tools` import and dispatches calls to it, so
/// the caller carries no router; the same implementer must serve that import
/// for loading to work, which is why `tool-loading-provider` exports both.
/// Admission follows `spawn`: the toolset's reach must fit inside the
/// caller's ceiling, and anything beyond it goes to a grantor and returns
/// `needs-approval` with a digest over the caller's identity and the
/// `toolset-spec`. Policy may ask for approval even within the ceiling,
/// since new code is new risk even when its reach is not.
@since(version = 0.5.0)
interface tool-loading {
    use spawn-types.{toolset-spec, admission-error};

    /// A toolset loaded into the caller. Dropping it unloads the toolset: the
    /// implementer stops offering its tools and stops its component. A call
    /// in progress is cancelled cooperatively; its effects are not rolled
    /// back. The implementer also unloads every toolset when the caller's
    /// store ends.
    resource loaded-toolset {
        /// The tool names under which the caller's `tools` import offers this
        /// toolset's definitions. Chosen by the implementer, which keeps them
        /// unique.
        get-names: async func() -> list<string>;
    }

    /// Check the spec against the ceiling and policy, start the toolset, and
    /// offer its tools through the caller's `tools` import. Resolves once
    /// they are offered.
    load: async func(spec: toolset-spec) -> result<loaded-toolset, admission-error>;
}
